Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, with naturalistic faces, candlelight, aged wood, and nineteenth-century costumes.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

A narrow, gaslit hall of dark timber. THE NARRATOR, a gaunt young man with slick black hair, hollow cheeks, and a plain black waistcoat, stands outside a bedroom door.

(SOUND: wind presses against shuttered windows; a clock ticks beyond the door)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD HOUSE DINING ROOM - DAY

Morning light lies across a worn dining table. MR. VALE, a thin old man in a gray night robe, sits with his back to the window; his pale blue eye is clouded by a milky film.

(SOUND: teaspoons click against china; a distant carriage rolls over cobbles)

THE NARRATOR places a cup before MR. VALE with careful, steady hands.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

MR. VALE turns. His filmed blue eye fixes on THE NARRATOR.

(MUSIC: a low bowed string note tightens beneath the room tone)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR, still in his black waistcoat, eases the bedroom latch open by a fraction. A closed dark lantern hangs from his hand.

(SOUND: latch metal barely scrapes; the house clock begins midnight chimes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

THE NARRATOR slips his head through the narrow opening, inch by inch.

(SOUND: cloth brushes wood; a floorboard settles)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man’s sleep.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

MR. VALE sleeps beneath a heavy bedcover. His gray hair spills across the pillow; shutters seal the room in blackness.

(SOUND: MR. VALE breathes softly; the clock ticks in the hall)

THE NARRATOR opens the lantern a hair. A thin ray reaches the bed, but MR. VALE'S eye remains closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD HOUSE DINING ROOM - DAY

THE NARRATOR serves breakfast to MR. VALE. He smiles broadly; MR. VALE, in his gray robe, peers down at his cup through the filmed eye.

(SOUND: a knife spreads butter; morning birds call beyond the window)

THE NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

MR. VALE
Well enough. Well enough.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

On the eighth night, THE NARRATOR turns the latch with agonizing slowness. His thumb slips on the lantern’s tin fastening.

(SOUND: the tin fastening snaps; MR. VALE'S bed ropes creak)

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

MR. VALE bolts upright in bed, gray robe clutched at his throat. His pale blue eye searches the pitch-black room.

(SOUND: a night cricket chirps once; wind murmurs in the chimney)

MR. VALE
Who’s there?

THE NARRATOR remains frozen in the doorway, lantern closed against his chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

MR. VALE emits a low, stifled groan.

(SOUND: MR. VALE'S groan fades into the ticking clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR opens the lantern a sliver. A dim thread of light falls directly onto MR. VALE'S open, clouded blue eye.

(SOUND: lantern hinge creaks; MR. VALE'S heartbeat begins as a muffled pulse)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it.

THE NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless. MR. VALE grips the bedcover, his eye unblinking in the ray.

(SOUND: heartbeat quickens beneath the ticking clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst.

THE NARRATOR suddenly throws the lantern wide and lunges into the room.

(SOUND: lantern clasp cracks open; MR. VALE gives one piercing shriek)

THE NARRATOR drags MR. VALE to the floor and hauls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: bedframe crashes down; the muffled heartbeat continues beneath it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

THE NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, one hand pressed to the still body beneath.

(SOUND: the heartbeat stops; silence swallows the room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDROOM - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR, sleeves rolled up over his thin forearms, lifts three floorboards beside the bed. A wooden tub stands nearby. He works in the darkness with grim precision.

(SOUND: saw teeth rasp through wood; rain begins against the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

THE NARRATOR lowers wrapped portions beneath the floor scantlings, then resets the planks flush.

(SOUND: floorboards knock into place; a distant bell tolls four)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR, black waistcoat buttoned and hair smoothed, opens the street door. Three constables stand under the dim streetlamp: OFFICER HART, broad and mustached; OFFICER BELL, lean and clean-shaven; and OFFICER CRANE, elderly and stiff-backed.

(SOUND: hard knocking ceases; rainwater drips from the officers’ capes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door.

THE NARRATOR gestures them inside with a courteous smile.

OFFICER HART
A shriek was heard during the night. We are deputed to search the premises.

THE NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I smiled,—for what had I now to fear?

INT. MR. VALE'S BEDROOM - PRE-DAWN

OFFICER HART, OFFICER BELL, and OFFICER CRANE inspect the bed, shutters, and undisturbed treasures. THE NARRATOR brings in four chairs.

(SOUND: boots cross the floorboards; rain taps steadily on the shutters)

THE NARRATOR places his own chair directly over the concealed planks and sits upon it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I bade them search—search well.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse.

The officers sit. THE NARRATOR answers with a quick smile while they chat quietly among themselves.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape softly; a small clock ticks with relentless regularity)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

THE NARRATOR'S smile tightens. He touches his temple.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone.

(SOUND: a low muffled heartbeat begins under the officers’ quiet conversation)

THE NARRATOR looks sharply at the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

THE NARRATOR speaks faster, hands fluttering over his knees. The officers remain calm.

(SOUND: heartbeat grows louder; rain and ticking recede beneath it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not.

THE NARRATOR rises and paces with heavy strides. He argues wildly with empty air, then seizes his chair.

(SOUND: chair legs grate across the boards; heartbeat pounds louder)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

THE NARRATOR swings the chair down onto the floorboards.

(SOUND: chair crashes; the heartbeat overwhelms the room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

THE NARRATOR drops to his knees and claws at the planks.

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!

NARRATOR
Here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

(SOUND: the heartbeat cuts dead; rain continues on the shutters)

THE NARRATOR stares at the torn floor as the three officers stand over him.

FADE OUT.

THE END
